<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice Frydman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Swami Bharatananda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice Frydman was a Polish-born engineer and spiritual writer who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 1930s, where he became a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and of Ramana Maharshi and took the name Swami Bharatananda. He drafted the 1939 constitution of the princely state of Aundh, which gave power to village councils. He later edited and translated Nisargadatta Maharaj's talks, published as I Am That.</p></div>
